{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"functional-patterns","title":"Currying, Composition & Memoization","group":"Advanced Concepts","order":50,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Functional techniques built from closures and higher-order functions — and staple interview implementations.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-right\"></i>Currying</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Currying</strong> — transforming <code>f(a, b, c)</code> into <code>f(a)(b)(c)</code> — a chain of single-argument functions. <em>Partial application</em> is the looser idea of fixing some arguments now and the rest later.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function curry(fn) {\n  return function curried(...args) {\n    return args.length &gt;= fn.length\n      ? fn.apply(this, args)\n      : (...more) =&gt; curried.apply(this, [...args, ...more]);\n  };\n}\nconst volume = (l, w, h) =&gt; l * w * h;\nconst cv = curry(volume);\nconsole.log(cv(2)(3)(4), cv(2, 3)(4), cv(2)(3, 4), cv(2, 3, 4));\n\nconst log = curry((level, scope, msg) =&gt; `[${level}] ${scope}: ${msg}`);\nconst warnDb = log(\"WARN\")(\"db\");\nconsole.log(warnDb(\"slow query\"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>24 24 24 24\n[WARN] db: slow query</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-infinity\"></i>Infinite currying: sum(1)(2)(3)()</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const sum = (a) =&gt; (b) =&gt; (b === undefined ? a : sum(a + b));\nconsole.log(sum(1)(2)(3)(4)());</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-right\"></i>Composition and pipe</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const compose = (...fns) =&gt; (x) =&gt; fns.reduceRight((acc, f) =&gt; f(acc), x);\nconst pipe = (...fns) =&gt; (x) =&gt; fns.reduce((acc, f) =&gt; f(acc), x);\n\nconst trim = (s) =&gt; s.trim();\nconst lower = (s) =&gt; s.toLowerCase();\nconst slugify = (s) =&gt; s.replace(/\\s+/g, \"-\");\n\nconsole.log(pipe(trim, lower, slugify)(\"  Hello Big World \"));\nconsole.log(compose(slugify, lower, trim)(\"  Hello Big World \"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hello-big-world\nhello-big-world</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>pipe</code> runs left → right (reads like a recipe); <code>compose</code> right → left (mirrors maths: <code>f(g(x))</code>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>Memoization</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function memoize(fn, keyFn = (...args) =&gt; JSON.stringify(args)) {\n  const cache = new Map();\n  return function (...args) {\n    const key = keyFn(...args);\n    if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);\n    const result = fn.apply(this, args);\n    cache.set(key, result);\n    return result;\n  };\n}\nlet calls = 0;\nconst slowSquare = (n) =&gt; { calls++; return n * n; };\nconst fastSquare = memoize(slowSquare);\nconsole.log(fastSquare(9), fastSquare(9), fastSquare(3), \"calls:\", calls);\n\nconst fib = memoize((n) =&gt; (n &lt; 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)));\nconsole.log(fib(90));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>81 81 9 calls: 2\n2880067194370816000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Memoize only <strong>pure</strong> functions. An unbounded cache is a memory leak — cap it (LRU) or key it with a <code>WeakMap</code> when arguments are objects.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tool\"></i>once, partial and friends</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const partial = (fn, ...preset) =&gt; (...later) =&gt; fn(...preset, ...later);\nconst greet = (greeting, name) =&gt; `${greeting}, ${name}!`;\nconsole.log(partial(greet, \"Welcome\")(\"Mei\"));\n\nconst not = (pred) =&gt; (...args) =&gt; !pred(...args);\nconst isEven = (n) =&gt; n % 2 === 0;\nconsole.log([1, 2, 3, 4].filter(not(isEven)));\n\nconst tap = (fn) =&gt; (x) =&gt; { fn(x); return x; };\nconsole.log([1, 2].map(tap((x) =&gt; console.log(\"saw\", x))));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Welcome, Mei!\n[ 1, 3 ]\nsaw 1\nsaw 2\n[ 1, 2 ]</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Implement a curry function.</summary><div><p>Return a function that collects arguments; when it has at least <code>fn.length</code> of them, call <code>fn</code>, otherwise return another function that appends further arguments (see the implementation above).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is memoization?</summary><div><p>Caching a function's results keyed by its arguments so repeated calls with the same input return instantly. It trades memory for speed and is only correct for pure functions.</p></div></details></section>"}